// Practice quiz: Stack vs heap and object references
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/classes-and-objects/stack-vs-heap-and-object-references

::o1 where things live::Inside a method\: int n \= 3; Point p \= new Point(); Where are n, p and the Point object? {
~All three are on the heap#Not quite. Local variables belong to the method frame on the stack.
~All three are on the stack#Not quite. Objects made with new live on the heap.
~n is on the stack; p and the Point object are both on the heap#Not quite. p is a local variable too; only the object it refers to is on the heap.
=n and p are in the method frame on the stack; the Point object is on the heap#Correct. Local variables live in the frame, and p holds a reference to the object on the heap.
}

::o1 method returns::A method returns. What happens to its local variables and the objects they referred to? {
~The locals and their objects are all deleted at once#Not quite. An object returned or stored elsewhere is still in use.
=The frame and its locals are gone; the objects stay on the heap while anything still reaches them#Correct. Removing a frame removes references, not objects.
~The locals stay until the program ends#Not quite. A frame lasts only as long as its method call.
~The objects move onto the stack#Not quite. Objects do not move between stack and heap.
}

::o2 shared array::int[] x \= \{1, 2\}; int[] y \= x; y[0] \= 9; What is x[0]? {
~1#Not quite. No copy of the array was made, so the change shows through x.
~A compile error#Not quite. Assigning one array variable to another is allowed.
=9#Correct. y \= x copies the reference, so both variables refer to one array.
~0#Not quite. Nothing reset the array; its first element was set to 9.
}

::o2 rebind::class Box \{ int size; \} Box a \= new Box(); Box b \= a; a \= new Box(); a.size \= 3; What is b.size? {
=0#Correct. b still refers to the first box; a was pointed at a new box before size was set.
~3#Not quite. Only the second box was changed.
~A NullPointerException#Not quite. b refers to a real object.
~A compile error#Not quite. Every line here is valid.
}

::o3 lost object::Box a \= new Box(); a \= new Box(); After the second line, how many Box objects can no longer be reached? {
~0#Not quite. The first box lost its only reference.
=1, the first box#Correct. Nothing refers to the first box any more, so it is eligible for garbage collection.
~2#Not quite. The second box is still reachable through a.
~1, the second box#Not quite. a now refers to the second box.
}

::o3 eligible::Which statement about garbage collection is true? {
~Setting a variable to null deletes the object immediately#Not quite. It removes one reference; reclaiming happens later, if at all.
~Calling System.gc() always frees memory at once#Not quite. It is only a request that the JVM may ignore.
~Every object is freed when the method that created it returns#Not quite. An object still reachable, for example one that was returned, stays.
=An object becomes eligible once no reference chain from running code reaches it; the JVM decides when, or whether, to reclaim it#Correct. Eligibility is about reachability, not timing.
}
